  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows widespread light rain \ngradually decreasing in coverage across east central Florida. GOES-\n19 satellite imagery and local observations indicate cloudy skies. \nAnalysis charts show a broad area of low pressure (~1010mb) over the \nsoutheast Gulf and southern Florida which is tracking northeast. \nTemperatures are currently in the mid 60s to low 70s with dew points \nin the low 60s to low 70s. Rain chances gradually decrease this \nevening with isolated to scattered showers (20-60% chance) forecast \novernight (mainly north of the Treasure Coast). A Flood Watch\nremains in effect until 1 AM Thursday. The latest hi-resolution\nguidance has little in the way of additional rainfall with most\nlikely rainfall amounts of up to T to 0.25\" and high end (90th\npercentile) amounts up to 0.1-0.5\". The potential exists for\nflooding of roadways and low lying areas if amounts exceed the \n90th percentile. Low temperatures in the mid 50s to low 60s are \nforecast north of I-4 with the upper 50s to mid 60s to the south.\n\nThursday-Friday...  Drier air is forecast Thursday into Friday \nbehind a backdoor cold front as flow aloft (250mb) becomes mostly \nzonal over east central Florida and as high pressure (~1024mb) at \nthe surface builds across the Deep South. However, lingering \nisolated showers are forecast north of Melbourne in the morning \nThursday before drier air filters in across east central Florida \nThursday afternoon into Friday. Expect breezy conditions with winds \nturning out of the west at 10-20mph with gusts to 25-30mph. Cooler \nbelow normal (~3-8F+ degrees) temperatures are forecast behind the \nfront with highs in the mid 60s to mid 70s Thursday and the low 60s \nto low 70s on Friday. Lows in the mid 40s to low 60s north of I-4 \nand the low to upper 50s are forecast to the south. \n\nSaturday-Tuesday... The dry weather is forecast to persist through \nSaturday as high pressure (~1016mb) builds east over the Deep South \nbefore moisture begins to increase Sunday and into next week. The \nLREF-ensemble clusters are in good agreement with the weather pattern \nthrough the next week, however, deterministic models diverge in the \npotential scenarios Sunday night into Monday. There is the potential \nfor two different scenarios to occur; the first is a trough/low \ndeveloping over the Gulf and tracking towards the state of Florida \nwhich would likely result in higher rainfall chances and totals for \neast central Florida. The second scenario is no trough/low \ndevelopment over the Gulf with a cold front moving east-southeast \nover east central Florida which would likely result in lower rain \nchances and rainfall totals. Northerly winds are forecast to turn \nonshore on Sunday, southwest on Monday, and north-northwest on \nTuesday. Cooler temperatures are expected to continue into the \nweekend before rising into the mid 60s to upper 70s on Sunday with \nseasonable highs in the low 70s to low 80s into next week. \nSeasonable to below normal low temperatures in the mid 40s to mid \n80s are forecast Saturday morning which are expected to gradually \nwarm to above normal into next week.",
